Q: How do you delete (or fill with specific values) a static n-dimension array? A: <p>The easiest and most effective way is to treat your fixed-dimension array as a new type.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;cstdio&gt;
#include &lt;array&gt;

const int ROWS = 3;
const int COLUMNS = 4;
typedef std::array&lt;std::array&lt;double, COLUMNS&gt;, ROWS&gt; my_array_t;

// this code will be missing from executable, it will run only during compilation
static constexpr my_array_t initMyArray ()
{
    my_array_t ret {};
    for (unsigned row = 0; row &lt; ret.size (); ++row)
        for (unsigned col = 0; col &lt; ret [row].size (); ++col)
            ret [row][col] = row * col; // or a constant, or whatever
    return ret;
}

// fill array with whatever you like during compilation (requires C++17)
constexpr my_array_t a = initMyArray ();

void printMyArray (const char *name, const my_array_t &amp;a)
{
    std::printf (&quot;Contents of array %s:\n&quot;, name);
    for (unsigned row = 0; row &lt; a.size (); ++row)
    {
        for (unsigned col = 0; col &lt; a [row].size (); ++col)
            std::printf (&quot;%f &quot;, a [row][col]);
        std::printf (&quot;\n&quot;);
    }
}

int main ()
{
    printMyArray (&quot;a&quot;, a);

    // create a new object on heap
    my_array_t *b = new my_array_t {};

    printMyArray (&quot;b&quot;, *b);

    // destroy the object
    delete b;

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to prevent DuplicateKeyException when using MongoDB upsert with optimistic concurrency (versioning)? A: <p>As you have already mentioned, the <code>DuplicateKeyException</code> can be used to detect that there was a document with the same id, but a version that does not fulfill the version part of your filter. This is a viable solution, though I'd also try to avoid using exceptions for normal program flow.</p>
<h1>Alternative 1: Update with aggregation pipeline</h1>
<p>There is an alternative that simplifies the filter, but complicates the update. Basically, you remove the version part from the filter and only filter for the id. MongoDB will do an update if there is a document with a matching id and insert the document if there is none.
The catch is that your update needs to decide whether it should update the property based on the current version of the document. You can do this with an <a href="https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/tutorial/update-documents-with-aggregation-pipeline/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">update with aggregation pipeline</a>. This means that instead of using regular update operators like <code>$set</code>, <code>$inc</code>, etc. you create a pipeline that is fed with the current state of the object and applies aggregation pipeline stages.</p>
<p>This is a powerful way of updating, because the pipeline can use multiple stages in the pipeline and assign the values dynamically with aggregation pipeline operators.</p>
<p>The following sample demonstrates the behavior:</p>
<p>For the sample, I have used this document:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>internal class UserVersioned
{
    [BsonRepresentation(BsonType.ObjectId)]
    public string Id { get; set; } = ObjectId.GenerateNewId().ToString();
    public int Version { get; set; } = 0;
    public string Name { get; set; } = &quot;&quot;;
}
</code></pre>
<p>The following code is used to update a document:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>var user = new UserVersioned
{
    Id = &quot;69cd32241e67ca2f1e8631ea&quot;,
    Name = &quot;Alice 1&quot;,
    Version = 1
};

var pipeline = new EmptyPipelineDefinition&lt;UserVersioned&gt;()
    .Set(u =&gt; new UserVersioned
    {
        Version = u.Version &gt;= user.Version ? u.Version : user.Version,
        Name = u.Version &gt;= user.Version ? u.Name : user.Name,
    });

var update = Builders&lt;UserVersioned&gt;.Update
    .Pipeline(pipeline);

var result = await coll.UpdateOneAsync(
    Builders&lt;UserVersioned&gt;.Filter.Eq(x =&gt; x.Id, user.Id),
    update,
    new UpdateOptions { IsUpsert = true });

Console.WriteLine($&quot;MatchedCount: {result.MatchedCount}, ModifiedCount: {result.ModifiedCount}, UpsertedId: {result.UpsertedId}&quot;);
</code></pre>
<p>What the code above does is the following:</p>
<ol>
<li>It creates a user object with the values that you want to update</li>
<li>It puts together an aggregation pipeline with a single <code>$set</code> stage that assigns the properties. If the version in currently stored document is greater or equal than the version that you want to use in your update, it uses the values that are already in the database instead of writing your changes.</li>
<li>It uses the pipeline in the update.</li>
<li>It performs the update with a filter for the id.</li>
</ol>
<p>This will upsert the document as expected and keep a newer version unchanged without throwing an exception. It also is just a single atomic request to the database. But, as you can recognize from the sample above, it takes a lot of typing for each property that requires testing thorougly. However, if you know LINQ and expressions very well, you could create a helper method for this.</p>
<h1>Alternative 2: Multi-document transactions</h1>
<p>Another alternative would be to use multi-document transactions (introduced with MongoDB 4.2) and perform several requests - if needed. In the transaction, you could follow this procedure:</p>
<ol>
<li>Upsert with FindAndModify with filter on the id that returns the previous version of the object.</li>
<li>Check the version of the previous document: if the version is equal to or greater than the version you wrote, roll back the transaction, else commit (also, if there has not been a previous version, commit).</li>
</ol>
<h1>Best practice</h1>
<p>As you can see, there are several alternatives to solve this. It depends on your scenario what the best solution is.</p>
<p>Alternative 1 is the most elegant and performant, but might be too sophisticated if concurrency issues are seldom and only a minor issue. For the other options, I think that the exception-based approach should be better in terms of performance - you only pay the performance hit if there really is a concurrency violation and not for each request.</p>
